    "body": "With global demand for missiles and missile defense surging, the US finds itself struggling to produce enough equipment to meet the Pentagon’s requirement — let alone that of foreign partners who rely on American systems. While there are efforts underway to increase production, there are still questions about whether its enough to help industry move as fast as needed.\nIn the latest edition of our look at missiles and missile defenses, Becca Wasser of Bloomberg Economics and Mark Cancian of the Center for International and Strategic Studies joined Breaking Defense Editor-in-Chief Aaron Mehta to discuss production challenges and the scary potential outcomes if the US and its partners can’t solve them.\nKeep an eye on future episodes here . You can catch part one here , and part two here .\nBreaking Defense Video\nThe US is racing to fix its missile production challenge. Will it work, and can partners help?\nWatch Now »\nWhy the Pentagon is asking for ‘anomalies’ as it plans for a long-term CR\nWatch Now »\nTrump’s carrier desire might not take flight, and a new twist for Navy shipbuilding\nWatch Now »\nCandidates are lining up to be the next top HASC Republican.\nWatch Now »\nThe real-world missile defense lessons from Ukraine and Iran — and how they matter for China\nWatch Now »\nA $1.1 trillion Pentagon budget plan sets up a fight on Capitol Hill\nWatch Now »\nThe Pentagon fails another audit. Can it turn things around by 2028?\nWatch Now »\nA new Air Force stunt plane, plus a shakeup for special operations\nWatch Now »\nNATO nations have a missile crisis. How did it happen, and can it be fixed?\nWatch Now »\nA Pentagon impeachment showdown, plus defense funding in limbo\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
